  "body": "This change clarifies the fact that the outline algorithm requires implementations to make any `hgroup` element in the source result in a multi-level heading in the generated outline.\n### Background\n\nThe first implementation of the outline algorithm that I added to the HTML checker didn’t produce a multi-level heading for any `hgroup` element in the resulting outline. Instead, it just took the first `h1`-`h6` descendant of the `hgroup` and put that into the outline and did nothing with any other `h1`-`h6` descendants (subheadings) the `hgroup` might have.\n\nI notice that @gsnedders [HTML 5 Outliner](https://gsnedders.html5.org/outliner/) and @dominykas [h5o - HTML5 Outliner ](https://h5o.github.io/) also only put the first `h1`-`h6` descendant of the `hgroup` into the outline and do nothing with any other `h1`-`h6` descendants (subheadings) the `hgroup` might have.\n\nA good example to test with is just [the HTML of the HTML spec itself](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/), which has this:\n\n``` html\n<hgroup>\n    <h1 class=allcaps>HTML</h1>\n    <h2 id=living-standard class=\"no-num no-toc\">Living Standard — Last Updated <span class=pubdate>5 August 2016</span></h2>\n</hgroup>\n```\n\nIn the rendered outline output, per spec that should result in something like either of the following:\n\n> HTML: Living Standard — Last Updated 5 August 2016\n> HTML (Living Standard — Last Updated 5 August 2016)\n\nThat is, with the outline showing the contents of the `h1` descendant of the `hgroup` as the primary heading, followed by the contents of the `h2` with some additional punctuation or something to indicate it is a secondary heading. (The spec doesn’t define any requirements about the actual rendering of the outline—it just defines what contents must be included in the outline.)\n\nI recently corrected the outline implementation the HTML checker to handle this case correctly; see the **Structural outline** output here:\n\nhttps://checker.html5.org/?showoutline=yes&doc=https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/\n\nHowever the fact that @gsnedders and @dominykas and I all seem to have (mis)read the spec in such a way as to ignore the hgroup-makes-multi-level-heading-in-outline requirement leads me to believe some clarification is needed in the spec to help any other implementors of the outline algorithm make sure they get it right.\n",
